(*
PortaBridgeNodeNrf — PortaBridgeNode, on the nRF52840, over the same real Thread
mesh the echo soak has been running on since 2026-07-11.

ONE FILE FOR BOTH nRF BOARDS, and that is a fact about the hardware rather than a
convenience: the DK and the PCA10059 dongle differ in console (uart0/VCOM vs SoC
USB-CDC), trigger pin (BUTTON1 P0.11 vs SW1 P1.06) and flash method (J-Link vs nRF
Util DFU) — every one of which is Kconfig or devicetree, NONE of which is nsl. This
file was briefly called ...NodeDK before the board on the bench turned out to be a
dongle; the name was the only thing that had to change. See
docs/BOARD_PORTS.md for the per-board table. rete#281: three DKs have
round-tripped 720,878 datagrams to `udp-echo6` on gw85224-01 and NONE of it reaches
porta, because nothing on nRF ever spoke the space protocol. This file is the item
that fixes that; items 2-5 of #281 (layout json, run.sh profile, collector env, heap
re-derivation) are the plumbing around it.

This file is PortaBridgeNode.nsl with FOUR differences and no others:
  1. the constants below address the real gateway over the mesh instead of
     loopback — the gw ML-EID and porta's :6969, not 127.0.0.1:16969;
  2. `chip` answers #nrf52840, so obs/build says which silicon reported. This is
     the point of the whole exercise: it is the FIRST non-Espressif row porta will
     have, and #281's argument is that two C6s is the entire current sample;
  3. `localPort` is 16970, the target twins' choice. The host twin's 16979 is a
     LOOPBACK workaround (porta serve takes 16969..16970 first when both ends run
     on one machine); on a board the gateway is a different host and the band is
     free, so this file agrees with PortaBridgeNodeC6 rather than with its own
     parent;
  4. `tick` prints one console line, for PortaBridgeNodeC6's difference-3 reason:
     porta cannot witness the node's own `misses`, so a leg that passes either way
     is not a test. On first bring-up this is also the only evidence available
     before any row reaches the store.
A change to the BRIDGING body of this file is a change to all three. That rule now
spans THREE files and is enforced by nothing — see rete#172, which already records
the C6 twin's header drifting out of step with its own body. Landing this file makes
#172 worse on purpose: the alternative was a fourth mechanism (a shared base class
or a constants record) invented before any DK node had run once.

THE ARENA IS THE RISK (#281 item 5). The rung-7 echo node ran config D at
OEVM_HEAP_SIZE=49152 with VM_STACK_INIT_WORDS=4096 — and on this part the VM stack
is fixed INSIDE the arena, which is the real boot-OOM. That node held no space, no
bridge, no Spans. The C6 twin needs 128 KB. The DK has 256 KB of RAM, so the arena
is the lever; boot with it raised and let the measurement earn it back down, rather
than guessing a smaller number and reading the OOM as a design fault.

MTD vs FTD: the soak nodes are MTDs, PortaBridgeNodeC6 is an FTD. The gw ML-EID is
correct either way and does not depend on the role (rung-7 note), so this file does
not care — but an MTD child has no OMR source address at all, which is why the
address below is mesh-local and must stay that way.

Dialect: `[ ... ] whileTrue:`, never `repeat`; state in instance slots so every
reaction block is a one-line self-send.
expect (console /dev/ttyACM0 @115200, once attached):
PORTA NODE READY
tick 1 misses 0 acked 0
*)
class PortaBridgeNodeNrf usingPlatform: platform net: net = (
  | private space private bridge private timers private ticks ::= 0.
    (* tuvm#107: the span table. ONE window per boot — see `main:args:`. *)
    private spans private capture private sTick private sCheckIn private sPublish private capturing ::= false. |
) (
  (* rete#241: `platform nodeId`, same one-liner as the C6 twin. #186 kept a literal
     here because host answered the slot-shaped "host" for every VM at once; d5b5545
     made a host VM name itself `<hostname>-NNNN`; rete#118 deleted that fallback. A
     host VM has no silicon, so it answers the OEVM_NODE_ID env value and the primitive
     fails when it is unset or empty. The gate (`tests/porta_sync_host.sh`) supplies a
     per-run id through it, so two concurrent runs no longer write into one porta space
     the way two devkits shared `c6-bench`. *)
  public nodeId    = ( ^ platform nodeId )
  (* gw85224-01's mesh-local EID, the same literal ThreadEchoNodeDK and
     PortaBridgeNodeC6 already use, read from the OTBR on 2026-06-28 and unchanged
     since. NOT the OMR address: there is no global IPv6 on this mesh and no DNS64,
     and an MTD child never SLAAC-configures an OMR address, so a send to the OMR
     prefix fails SILENTLY — the rung-7 lesson, and it cost a bench session. *)
  gwAddr    = ( ^ 'fdc5:9109:9afe:97ea:9541:d963:f8d9:8567' )
  gwPort    = ( ^ 6969 )
  (* 16970 — see difference 3 in the header. The host twin's 16979 exists only
     because porta serve and the node share one machine under
     tests/porta_sync_host.sh; here they do not. *)
  localPort = ( ^ 16970 )
  cadence   = ( ^ 2000 )   (* ms between check-ins *)
  (* Build provenance, so an agent can tell which code is on which node. `chip`
     is a source constant because it is a build fact; `build` (obs/build) carries
     the string #74 gave the node to name its own build (git describe + timestamp).
     `sdk` and `image` are still OMITTED — #74 covers only the build id, not those
     — and absence means "the node cannot say", which is honest and is not a
     default. #nrf52840 is the first value here that is not an Espressif part. *)
  chip      = ( ^ #nrf52840 )

  public main: platform2 args: args = (
    | sock tickChan |
    space:: platform space.
    sock:: net udpBindPort: self localPort.
    sock == nil ifTrue: [ ^ self fail: 'bind' ].
    (* The ONE Spans for this image, minted BEFORE the bridge because the bridge
       registers its own six slots at construction. Two instances would both
       allocate from 2 and silently share samples. *)
    spans:: platform Spans new.
    bridge:: platform PortaBridge
      socket: sock space: space id: self nodeId peer: self gwAddr port: self gwPort
      spans: spans.
    bridge subscribeStream: 'tel/log'.
    bridge subscribeStream: 'sys/reset'.

    (* rete#273 D2: this node's own promise about its radio — telCad and how many
       consecutive misses it tolerates. 16 and not the fleet default of 8 because
       porta refuses to judge a budget below its own 30 s sweep tick (D2.1), and
       2 s x 8 = 16 s is; 2 s x 16 = 32 s is judged. The 2 s is bench turnaround,
       not a claim about the radio. appCad stays at the bridge default (x 8). *)
    bridge liveness: self cadence misses: 16.

    (* tuvm#107 landed, so the span cells fit the 256 B cap and `diag/**` is in the
       bridge's outbound cell scan. No subscribeStream: here — these are CELLS.

       ONE window per boot, closed by an event this node can name for itself: the
       first tick on which the gateway has ACKED something (`bridge acked > 0`), so
       the window is exactly "boot until the round trip is proven". No period, no
       tunable — a repeating window would need one, and there is no data yet to
       choose it from. After the window the cells stop changing, `changedSince:`
       stops returning them, and the steady-state wire cost is zero.

       Remote control of the window is a different question, blocked on tuvm#159:
       porta may write only goal/** and dbg/req, so it cannot drive diag/spans/ctl. *)
    (* `spans` was minted above, with the bridge, which holds slots 2-7 for its own
       six-way decomposition of checkIn; these three land at 8-10. *)
    sTick::     spans register: #jobTick.     (* the whole timer -> publish job *)
    sCheckIn::  spans register: #checkIn.     (* pull + push, the network round trip *)
    sPublish::  spans register: #publish.     (* the health and log writes *)
    capture::   platform SpanCapture onSpace: space spans: spans.
    capture start.
    capturing:: true.
    (space channel: 'diag/spans/ctl')
      put: '' value: (platform Record tag: #SpanCtl with: #state -> #running).

    (* porta's writes land as ordinary cell writes, so the echo is an ordinary
       reaction — there is no protocol handler anywhere in this node. *)
    (space channel: 'goal/name') whenever: '' do: [ :v | self nameChanged: v ].

    (* rete#312: `dbg/req` profile requests are the kernel's (Kernel>>profiler,
       installed by the Supervisor before main:); this node only dispatches
       `sys/timer`, which is what closes the window. *)

    (* rete#259: the bridge owns obs/build; #man rides iff the image carries a
       manifest pin (rete#243). *)
    bridge publishBuild: self chip.

    timers:: platform timers.     (* hold ONE instance — it owns the pending registry *)
    space whenever: 'sys/timer' do: [ :e | timers dispatch: e ].
    tickChan:: space channel: 'sys/tick'.
    tickChan whenever: '' do: [ :m | self tick ].
    (* rete#231 D1: the KERNEL owns the period now. The app still owns what a tick
       DOES — its reaction on this channel is unchanged — but the registration is
       the bridge's, so the deadline #215's observer reads from
       timer_target_snapshot exists even for an app that declares no timer of its
       own, and a heartbeat this node stops emitting when it wedges is a heartbeat
       whose absence means something. The bridge is HANDED this instance: two
       Timers alias each other's tokens and drop every dispatch silently. *)
    bridge startTicking: timers on: tickChan.

    'PORTA NODE READY' println.
    [ true ] whileTrue: [
      platform pumpEvents.        (* blocks until the cadence timer fires *)
      space drain ].
    ^ 0
  )

  (* One check-in, then one report. In that order: applying goal/** first means the
     health this node publishes describes the state porta just asked for, not the
     one before it. *)
  tick = (
    ticks:: ticks + 1.
    spans begin: sTick.
    spans begin: sCheckIn.
    bridge checkIn.
    spans end: sCheckIn.
    spans begin: sPublish.
    self publishHealth.
    space out: 'tel/log' value:
      (platform Record tag: #Log with: #lvl -> #info
                                 with: #txt -> ('tick ' , ticks printString)).
    spans end: sPublish.
    (* jobTick closes BEFORE the window does, so the tick that ends the window is
       itself measured — otherwise the last sample would be the one missing. *)
    spans end: sTick.
    (* difference 4 — the console line. `misses` and `acked` are the two numbers
       that separate "the mesh is up and porta answers" from "the mesh is up and
       porta does not", and neither is visible from the gateway side. *)
    ('tick ' , ticks printString , ' misses ' , bridge misses printString
             , ' acked ' , bridge acked printString) println.
    self closeWindowIfAcked.
    ^ self
  )

  (* tuvm#107: writing #stopped is what makes SpanCapture publish the per-slot cells
     and the { n, ms } summary; they ride the NEXT check-in's push. Guarded by
     `capturing` because #stopped while already stopped is ignored by SpanCapture,
     but the cell write itself is not free and this runs every tick forever. *)
  closeWindowIfAcked = (
    capturing ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
    bridge acked > 0 ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
    capturing:: false.
    (space channel: 'diag/spans/ctl')
      put: '' value: (platform Record tag: #SpanCtl with: #state -> #stopped).
    ^ self
  )

  nameChanged: v = ( space put: 'obs/name' value: v. ^ self )

  (* LIVENESS AND DELIVERY ONLY (design D2, 2026-08-04). heapFree/heapLargest/overruns
     left this record — not duplicated elsewhere, DELETED from here, because every one
     of them already lives at sys/metrics (RECORDS §4.4), which the bridge now
     publishes on every check-in. Two overlapping health records is how obs/health
     came to have three mutually inconsistent definitions: 3 fields in the conformance
     target, 7 in RECORDS, 8 actually on the wire.

     wakes/pollTimeouts carry the tick count and the bridge's missed replies until the
     mode record gives them a stricter meaning.

     streamDropped STAYS. It counts information lost on the way OUT, which is the
     number that says the observability itself is lying — a delivery fact about this
     node's reporting, not a VM internal. *)
  publishHealth = (
    | pairs |
    pairs:: platform OrderedCollection new.
    pairs add: #up -> platform monotonicMillis.
    (* `bridge bootId` and NOT the literal 1, and not `platform bootId` either:
       this is the same latched value the push envelope carries, so the record
       and the envelope cannot disagree. They did — rete#211: #162 made bootId a
       real persisted counter and moved the envelope over, but every app's
       publishHealth kept the constant, so obs/health answered `bid 1` forever
       while the envelope beside it said 38. obs/health is the ONLY place the
       boot id reaches a path, so that constant was the whole of the space's
       answer to "which boot is this?". 0 here is truthful, not a failure: the
       posix backend has no persistent identity unless OEVM_BOOTID_FILE is set. *)
    pairs add: #bid -> bridge bootId.
    pairs add: #wak -> ticks.
    pairs add: #pto -> bridge misses.
    pairs add: #sdr -> bridge dropped.
    (* rete#199 — REFUSED, and it is not `pto`. A node the gateway is uniformly
       rejecting gets a reply to every exchange, so `pto` reads 0 the whole way to
       its death; without this field the record is identical for a healthy node and
       for one that has been shouting into a closed door since boot. This is the
       one number that separates "the gateway is unreachable" from "the gateway
       refuses me", and they are different problems with different fixes. *)
    pairs add: #ref -> bridge refused.
    (* rete#232 rider — see PortaBridgeNodeC6: a counted-and-dropped inbound
       entry is a PERMANENT loss under porta's replay guard, so it must be
       visible. *)
    pairs add: #rej -> bridge rejected.
    space put: 'obs/health' value:
      (platform Record tag: #Health fields: (platform Map fromPairs: pairs)).
    ^ self
  )

  fail: msg = ( msg println. ^ 1 )
) : (
  public packageUsing: manifest = ( ^ self new )
)
